[Music]  the english language is strange when  it's raining cats and dogs we're not  literally talking about freak  meteorological events unusual sayings  like this one are everywhere and many of  them have truly bizarre origins did you  know that going berserk may have come  from viking war rituals and out of the  frying pan into the fire could be about  burning someone at the stake  if you want to know the unusual stories  behind our common expressions well  you're in the right place in this video  let's look at some more curious origins  of popular sayings  going cold turkey  why is quitting something suddenly  cooled going cold turkey what do these  birds have to do with kicking an  addictive habit this saying comes from  victorian london it can be found in a  satirical magazine which includes a  fictional story about a man who wrote a  relative out of his will after she  served him a cold turkey for christmas  soon the phrase emerged giving someone  the cold turkey treatment it was related  to the idea of cutting someone off  entirely but curiously today is used in  the context of addictive substances like  alcohol or drugs although no one is  quite sure how it made this jump  give a man a fish and you fed him once  teach mad fish and you fed him for a  lifetime  this proverb is about the value of  learning a new skill and how it  encourages self-reliance there's a  common misconception to this one that it  came from ancient china although many  proverbs are indeed attributed to  chinese philosophers this one is not one  of them instead it is neither ancient  nor was written by a man it first  appears in an 1819 novel by british  author ann isabella ritchie in her book  mrs diamond one of the characters says  quote if you give a man a fish he is  hungry again in an hour if you teach a  man to catch fish you do him a good turn  the myth that this saying is from  ancient china seems to have sprung up in  the 1960s the first to have made this  claim was an american magazine the  rotarian and it popularized the idea  that this came from the far east  since then the assumption has stuck  it's raining cats and dogs  this expression is used to describe  heavy rainfall like really heavy  rainfall as with a lot of phrases this  one came from jolly old england a nation  known for its miserable weather believe  it or not animals raining from the sky  is a documented meteorological  phenomenon but not with cats and dogs  but fish in the central american  republic of honduras there's a local  festival known as luva depeches  literally translating to fish rain in  the wet months of may and june fish  actually fall from the sky to which the  locals welcome with a carnival  scientists have documented this bizarre  natural phenomenon and have discovered  that it's a result of water spouts small  tornadoes that develop over water  they're able to pick up water and scoop  any unfortunate marine knife in their  path and then rain them both down onto  nearby land  okay we've established that raining fish  is actually a thing but what about cats  and dogs there are no reported cases of  this actually happening no tornadoes are  sweeping through pet stores at least as  far as i'm aware  turns out the whole reigning cats and  dogs thing has a completely separate and  unrelated story behind it some  folklorists say it came from norse  mythology where odin's dogs gary and  frecky were able to summon the wind  at the same time it was understood that  cats could magically influence the  weather  especially rain so raining cats and dogs  could mean that it's both windy  and rainy personally i'm not so sure  about this one it's raining rain and  wind  perhaps not also the descendants of  norse languages today have their own  uniquely strange idioms for bad weather  in swedish it's raining little devils  in norwegian it's raining she trolls and  my favorite danish it's reigning  shoemaker's apprentices thank you  scandinavia you never fail to amaze  okay let's get back on track another  leading theory is that it's actually  based on a greek expression in greek to  say something is beyond belief it's  catadoctor so if it's raining outside  like you won't believe it's raining  catadoxa  some linguists believe that the english  speakers misunderstood this greek word  and catadoxa mutated somehow into cats  and dogs  as it stands no one quite knows the  origin of this saying so let's take a  rain check on this one for now  with great power comes great  responsibility if the origin of this one  seems familiar to you then you may be a  fan of spider-man these are the famous  last words that uncle ben uttered to  peter parker although when it first  appeared in 1962 it was spoken by the  narrator  this saying has become so tied with the  marvel superhero that has been nicknamed  the parker principle however this  memorable saying about the  accountability of power was actually not  devised by spider-man's creators stan  lee and steve ditko instead it has a far  longer history they may have got it from  u.s president franklin d roosevelt who  in 1945 said quote in a democratic world  as in a democratic nation power must be  linked with responsibility  two years prior in 1943 british prime  minister winston churchill uttered a  very similar phrase quote the price of  greatness is responsibility  but he in turn may have borrowed it from  a french revolution address where in  1793 it was declared that where there is  power there must also be responsibility  this memorable phrase is intertwined  with the rise of democracy in the  western world so how spider-man's uncle  ben got all the credit is quite the  mystery  go berserk  to go berserk is to get angry  this word came to english from the  vikings and was originally reserved for  the most ferocious warrior of all the  berserker  the sagas tell us that before a battle  these warriors would enter a frenzy  trance they'd foam at the mouth yell  furiously and be unable to tell friend  from foe  other legends tell us that these  berserkers were even capable of reaching  superhuman strength believed to be  immune to both fire and steel  viking historians have suggested that  these warriors were able to enter such a  state through a type of ritual that  induced a manic hysteria  others say that they may have consumed  psychedelic substances like certain  mushrooms or seeds alternatively others  have simply said that they just drank so  much beer and they were off their faces  the entire time  interestingly where go berserk came from  scandinavia the related phrase running a  mock may have come from the far east a  mark is indonesian in origin it's a word  used to describe a sudden outburst of  violence in many folk religions it was  said that the demon tiger hantu bellian  had possessed the person and caused them  to go mad forgetting who they were and  what they were doing british explorer  captain james cook once documented an  instance of a mock he described how man  once went about quote killing and  maiming villagers and animals in a  frenzied attack  the word amok then entered the english  language and was used to describe  chaotic individuals  avoid like the plague the meaning of  this phrase is pretty simple is all to  do with staying away from something or  someone at all costs for most of human  history there's one disease that  everybody wanted to avoid the bubonic  plague for victims painful sores known  as buboes would erupt on the body if the  disease entered the bloodstream then  death would swiftly follow  the first person to write this phrase  down was possibly saint jerome after  translating the bible into latin he  offered this advice quote avoid as you  would the plague a clergyman who was  also a man of business  fortunately for saint jerome he never  actually had to live through a plague  epidemic it would be roughly 120 years  after his death when the roman empire  under justinian suffered a horrific  outbreak of bubonic plague spreading  across the mediterranean and possibly as  far as china this plague is believed to  have killed anywhere between 15 to 100  million people  but this was dwarfed by another outbreak  some 800 years later the black death  between 1346 to 1353  the same disease believed to have come  from central asia this time killed  roughly 75 to 200 million people  worldwide claiming the lives of a third  of medieval europe's population  you can see why this phrase a void like  the plague caught on and spread it first  appeared in english in 1647  shortly before another devastating  bubonic plague was to break out in the  city of london  nowadays whilst global pandemics are  certainly not behind us it's perhaps  reassuring to know that bubonic plague  is now treatable with antibiotics  out of the frying pan  into the fire  from one dark saying to another this  phrase is used when something goes from  bad to worse it's believed to have come  from reformation england and may be to  do with burning people at the stake it  first appeared in a heated religious  debate between catholic theologian  thomas more and protestant reformer  william tyndale thomas moore argues that  his rival has dabbed himself to hell  with his controversial statements he  writes that his opponent has quote by  his false reproach of trickery conveyed  himself out of the frypan into the fire  in other words such blasphemy would not  only land you on a burning stake but  also land you in the fires of hell  but why frying pans  well there was a contemporary christian  belief that one of the many ways that  demons tortured people in hell was by  cooking them in a giant frying pan  now i know it sounds a bit strange but  it was a genuine fear held at the time  if you've ever read dante's inferno  you'll know that there are a lot more  strange punishments out there  so out of the frying pan and into the  fire only issue with this one is that it  really should be the other way round  from the fire the steak into the frying  pan hell  either way in the end william tyndale  really did end up being burnt at the  stake but this was only after henry viii  beheaded thomas moore to mix my  expressions i guess you could say that  he got a taste of his own medicine  well there you have it another  collection of stories behind some of our  most popular sayings we've covered a lot  of ground from berserk vikings to  bubonic plagues if there are any sayings  that you'd like to see covered let me  know down in the comments below  in the meantime consider checking out my  previous video on this very topic  but for now i think we'll leave it there  although there are still plenty more  sayings out there just waiting to be  explored  hey thanks for watching i really hope  you enjoyed this one  there were so many interesting sayings  left from the last video that i just had  to make a follow-up even still there's  plenty more left in the tank  if you'd like to see more why not  subscribe a like and a comment also go a  really long way  anyway i look forward to seeing you next  time  goodbye  [Music]

